1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,280
I was born in Paris, at the heart of my cradle, the Marcel-Lenoir gallery in Saint-Germain-des-Prés,

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:11,360
then I grew up within the majestic setting of the 18th-century Montricoux castle and its 12th–13th-century Templar tower.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:18,400
And indeed, the wonderful story is that one day my father arrived at the gallery as casually as could be,

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,220
saying, “I bought the Montricoux castle.”

5
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:23,400
My mother said to him, “This is a joke.”

6
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:27,500
“No, no, not at all, I’m back with all the signed papers, there’s no turning back now.”

7
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:30,100
because creating the museum was non-negotiable.

8
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,260
We buy the castle, we establish it, and we move there.

9
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:34,920
At first, they juggled the gallery,

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:38,340
but afterward, the castle became so consuming

11
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:43,620
that my parents gave up their parallel life as art dealers in Paris

12
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:48,720
to devote themselves entirely to the all-consuming Marcel-Lenoir,

13
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:50,660
who drew love toward himself.

14
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,239
And I must say that we try our very best to convey his work

15
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:57,740
as faithfully as possible, both

16
00:00:58,140 --> 00:00:59,880
with the awareness of portraying the man

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,040
he was, as well as the artist.

18
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:03,620
And indeed, here at the museum,

19
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,640
as in all museums, we care deeply about

20
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,700
presenting his art,

21
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,340
presenting his trajectory through every possible means

22
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:11,880
because I have the

23
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,040
feeling that my role

24
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:16,280
is also to pass on

25
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:17,720
the art of Marcel-Lenoir,

26
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:19,960
as museums do, through writings,

27
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,600
articles in scholarly journals,

28
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:23,940
online, on the Internet, or in print,

29
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:26,220
in

30
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,980
national and international exhibitions.

31
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,340
And all of this carries us into something extraordinary,

32
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,780
leading us, like him, to live a vibrant life,

33
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:36,720
because in the end, we never truly leave it,

34
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:38,220
we become lost within it,

35
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:40,640
my parents were prisoners of the labyrinth,

36
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,020
and now they have drawn me back into it as well,

37
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:43,160
and I do not want to leave it.

38
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:45,080
And I am not afraid to say

39
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,720
that we are connected

40
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,020
with Madeleine and Marcel-Lenoir.

41
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,840
We may sound a little eccentric saying this,

42
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:52,660
but they whisper to us,

43
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:54,240
they whisper things to us,

44
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,820
they inspire my writing when I take up my pen.

45
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,560
There are certain things, yes, I know they accompany us,

46
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:03,800
they are here, and I hope they are happy.

47
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:05,800
I believe they are happy nonetheless, I hope so.

48
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:11,120
And after all, this is our past, our present, and our future.
